
  - [1mJust the userId.[0m [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m / [38;5;153msetUserId()[39m / [38;5;153mclear()[39m. Smallest thing that satisfies "track who logged in". If a
    later form needs the username too, you widen the interface then — cheap while there's one consumer, annoying once
    there are five.
  - [1mA session object.[0m [38;5;153mgetSession()[39m returns [38;5;153m{ userId, username, loginAt }[39m, plus [38;5;153misLoggedIn()[39m. Costs a few more lines now
    and answers the questions forms actually ask ("show me the current user", "am I logged in?") without another round.
    Locks in a stored JSON shape you'd need a migration for if it changes.
  - [1mSession plus change notification.[0m Adds [38;5;153msubscribe(fn)[39m so UI updates when login state changes. Only worth it if
    something needs to re-render on logout in another tab; otherwise it's machinery with no caller.

  [1mI'd take the session object.[0m You've already told me other forms are coming, and [38;5;153misLoggedIn[39m is the question nearly
  every one of them asks. Notification is the YAGNI cut — no consumer exists, and it's additive later.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What should the shared session module expose? → Session object (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Fourth: how the page runs. This decides the module story — the session module has to be loadable by both [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and
  the later forms, and there's no bundler here.

  If you open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m straight from disk ([38;5;153mfile://[39m), ES modules are blocked by CORS and the module has to be a plain
  global script. If it's served over HTTP, [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m with real imports is the better structure. (Note
  [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m uses CommonJS [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m, which the browser can't load either way — that directory looks unrelated to
  the page.)
